<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintana Roo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintana Roo is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast. It holds Maya sites such as Tulum, Cobá and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzibanche">Dzibanche</a>, and was long a stronghold of rebel Maya during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> War. Made a federal territory in 1902, it became a state in 1974 and is now a centre of tourism around Cancún.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quintana-roo.jpg" alt="Quintana Roo" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Quintana_Roo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quintana Roo is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s easternmost state, on the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, with a coast on the Caribbean Sea to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> to the north. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> to the west, Yucatán to the northwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a> to the south. Its capital is Chetumal, and it is divided into 11 municipalities. The state includes the city of Cancún, the islands of Cozumel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isla-mujeres">Isla Mujeres</a>, the town of Bacalar and the Sian Ka'an biosphere reserve, and it has four generalised ecosystems: tropical forest, savanna, mangrove and coral reef. Its exposed position makes it frequently subject to severe hurricanes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was long part of Yucatán and shared its history. From about 2000 BCE the Maya transformed it into an agricultural forest, managing selected trees, and archaeological evidence shows ancient home gardens, managed tree plots and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> groves. Ancient human remains have been found in submerged caves and sinkholes near Tulum, including a woman who lived at least 9,900 years ago, found in the Chan Hol cave in 2016, one of the oldest human skeletons found on the American continent.</p><p>Spanish colonisation affected the region from the 16th century. With the Caste War of Yucatán, which began in the 1840s, all non-Indigenous peoples were driven from the region, and the independent Maya nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-carrillo-puerto-quintana-roo">Chan Santa Cruz</a>, based on what is now the town of Felipe Carrillo Puerto, kept considerable independence for decades, with separate trade and treaty relationships with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">British Honduras</a>, now Belize.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> created the Territory of Quintana Roo by decree on 24 November 1902, naming it after Andrés Quintana Roo, an early patriot of the Mexican Republic. The Mexican Federal Army subdued most of the Maya population during the 1910s. In 1913 the area was declared part of Yucatán again, but in 1915 it became a separate territory once more, and on 8 October 1974 it was granted statehood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the last quarter of the 20th century tourism, above all the development of Cancún, drove rapid growth in the north of the state, while the south kept a traditional economy of forestry and wood extraction. Many migrants have come from Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. The state holds the Maya sites of Chacchoben, Cobá, Kohunlich, Muyil, Tulum, Xel-Há, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-gervasio">San Gervasio</a> and Xcaret.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quintana Roo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today it is held by the extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, Belize, western Honduras and the Mexican states of Tabasco, Chiapas, <b>Quintana Roo</b>, Campeche and Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Popol Vuh belongs to the Kʼicheʼ people of Guatemala, one of the Maya peoples, who also live in the Mexican states of Chiapas, Campeche, Yucatan and <b>Quintana Roo</b> and in parts of Belize, Honduras and El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The modern city of Chetumal, capital of <b>Quintana Roo</b>, was founded in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/chetumal">Chetumal</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now a popular archaeological site in <b>Quintana Roo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/coba">Cobá</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay in the forests of east-central Yucatán, in what is now <b>Quintana Roo</b>, with Tihosuco and Ichmul among its main towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/cochuah">Cochuah</a></li><li><blockquote>The snake-head emblem dynasty of Kaan is first firmly attested at Dzibanche in southern <b>Quintana Roo</b> in the 5th and 6th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a></li></ul></section>
